{
  "title": "Where did you go yesterday?",
  "title_target": "Dün ne yaptın?",
  "goal": "Talk about what has already happened with the past suffix *-di*, including with nouns and adjectives.",
  "bekal": [
    "forming *geldim, geldin, geldi, geldik, geldiniz, geldiler*",
    "choosing between the eight forms *-dı, -di, -du, -dü, -tı, -ti, -tu, -tü*",
    "saying \"I was tired\" and \"there was\" with *-ydi* and *vardı*"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "Dialogue — Dün akşam",
      "intro": "Morning in Fatma Hanım's kitchen. Budi has just woken up; Emre is already on his second tea.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "Emre:",
          "text": "Günaydın! Dün akşam nerede kaldın?",
          "id": "Good morning! Where were you last night?",
          "literal": "good-morning! yesterday evening where stayed-you?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Sultanahmet'e gittim. Çok yürüdüm!",
          "id": "I went to Sultanahmet. I walked so much!",
          "literal": "Sultanahmet-to went-I. very walked-I!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Emre:",
          "text": "Ayasofya'yı gördün mü?",
          "id": "Did you see Hagia Sophia?",
          "literal": "Hagia-Sophia-(object) saw-you question?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Gördüm ama içeri girmedim. Sıra çok uzundu.",
          "id": "I saw it, but I didn't go in. The line was really long.",
          "literal": "saw-I but inside not-entered-I. line very long-was."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Emre:",
          "text": "Anladım. Peki akşam ne yedin?",
          "id": "I see. So what did you eat in the evening?",
          "literal": "understood-I. well evening what ate-you?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Bir balıkçıda balık ekmek yedim. Harikaydı.",
          "id": "I had a fish sandwich at a fish stall. It was amazing.",
          "literal": "a fish-seller-at fish bread ate-I. wonderful-was."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Emre:",
          "text": "Kaça?",
          "id": "How much?",
          "literal": "how-much-for?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Yüz elli lira. Pahalı mıydı?",
          "id": "A hundred and fifty lira. Was that expensive?",
          "literal": "hundred fifty lira. expensive question-was?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Emre:",
          "text": "Turist fiyatı! Ama sorun değil, güzeldi değil mi?",
          "id": "Tourist price! But never mind, it was good, wasn't it?",
          "literal": "tourist price-its! but problem not, nice-was not question?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Çok güzeldi. Sonra vapurla döndüm.",
          "id": "It was really good. Then I came back by ferry.",
          "literal": "very nice-was. then ferry-with returned-I."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Emre:",
          "text": "Yorgun musun şimdi?",
          "id": "Are you tired now?",
          "literal": "tired question-you now?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Dün çok yorgundum, bugün iyiyim.",
          "id": "I was very tired yesterday; today I'm fine.",
          "literal": "yesterday very tired-was-I, today good-I."
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**The person endings are different from the ones you know.** The *-di* form uses the second set: *-m, -n, —, -k, -niz, -ler*. That's why \"I came\" is *geldim*, not *geldiyim*. This set is also used for the conditional later on.",
        "**Adjectives can go into the past too.** *Yorgunum* (I'm tired) → *yorgundum* (I was tired). *Güzel* → *güzeldi*. *Var* → *vardı*, *yok* → *yoktu*. After a vowel, a *y* is inserted: *harika* → *harikaydı*.",
        "**Balık ekmek** is a grilled-fish sandwich sold on the Eminönü waterfront — Istanbul's most famous street food, and the simplest noun + noun pattern there is: fish + bread.",
        "**Pahalı mıydı?** shows an order worth noticing: adjective, then the question particle, then the past suffix. Turkish stacks them in exactly that order, and never the other way round."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The past tense -di",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The past suffix has **eight forms** — four from vowel harmony, times two from consonant harmony. That sounds like a lot, but both are rules you've been using since unit 5."
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Stem ends in",
            "Vowel a, ı",
            "Vowel e, i",
            "Vowel o, u",
            "Vowel ö, ü"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "soft letter",
              "al**dı**m",
              "gel**di**m",
              "ol**du**m",
              "gör**dü**m"
            ],
            [
              "hard letter (FiSTiKÇi ŞaHaP)",
              "bak**tı**m",
              "git**ti**m",
              "unut**tu**m",
              "öp**tü**m"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sub",
          "text": "Second-set person endings"
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "",
            "gelmek",
            "gitmek",
            "yapmak",
            "görmek"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "ben",
              "gel**dim**",
              "git**tim**",
              "yap**tım**",
              "gör**düm**"
            ],
            [
              "sen",
              "gel**din**",
              "git**tin**",
              "yap**tın**",
              "gör**dün**"
            ],
            [
              "o",
              "gel**di**",
              "git**ti**",
              "yap**tı**",
              "gör**dü**"
            ],
            [
              "biz",
              "gel**dik**",
              "git**tik**",
              "yap**tık**",
              "gör**dük**"
            ],
            [
              "siz",
              "gel**diniz**",
              "git**tiniz**",
              "yap**tınız**",
              "gör**dünüz**"
            ],
            [
              "onlar",
              "gel**diler**",
              "git**tiler**",
              "yap**tılar**",
              "gör**düler**"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Negation still uses *-me/-ma* before the tense suffix: *gelmedim* (I didn't come), *yapmadım*, *görmedim*. Questions still use the separate particle, this time AFTER the past suffix: *geldin mi?*, *yaptınız mı?*, *gelmedi mi?*"
        },
        {
          "type": "kotak",
          "title": "Why gitmek becomes gittim",
          "items": [
            "The stem *git-* ends in *t*, one of the hard letters — so the suffix becomes *-ti*. The result is two t's side by side: *git-tim*. That's correct, and it's spelled that way.",
            "Compare the *-yor* form, where the *t* SOFTENS instead, because the suffix starts with a vowel: *gidiyorum*. Same word, two directions of change — both decided by the first letter of the suffix."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "I used to be ..., there was ...",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The past suffix can also attach to nouns and adjectives, to say something **was that way**. The form is the same; you just need a linking *y* after a vowel."
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Now",
            "Past",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "öğrenciyim",
              "öğrenci**ydim**",
              "I was a student"
            ],
            [
              "yorgunum",
              "yorgun**dum**",
              "I was tired"
            ],
            [
              "güzel",
              "güzel**di**",
              "it was nice"
            ],
            [
              "harika",
              "harika**ydı**",
              "it was great"
            ],
            [
              "var",
              "var**dı**",
              "there was"
            ],
            [
              "yok",
              "yok**tu**",
              "there wasn't"
            ],
            [
              "değil",
              "değil**di**",
              "it wasn't"
            ],
            [
              "evde",
              "evde**ydim**",
              "I was at home"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Notice *yoktu*: since *yok* ends in *k* — a hard letter — the suffix becomes *-tu*, not *-du*. The Fıstıkçı Şahap rule works here exactly as it does with verbs."
        },
        {
          "type": "sub",
          "text": "Time words for storytelling"
        },
        {
